WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.440
I still get that, like, people are like

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.420
I'm not sitting in the
front row at your show.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.710
And I'm like, no, no, no, don't worry

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.180
I only roast volunteers.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.310
You're not gonna be a
sitting duck at my shows.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:09.240
Is that true?

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.210
When I do my shows, I'll do my proper act

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.150
whatever I'm working on
for 30 or 40 minutes.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.180
And then I'll say, all
right, I need volunteers

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.890
who wants to come up here and get roasted?

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.340
House lights go on,
people stand up, you know,

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.260
and I'll say like anybody pregnant

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:27.150
or disabled, raise your hand.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.340
And if people try to
point to other people,

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:30.510
I don't take that.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.343
Right. Right.

00:00:31.343 --> 00:00:32.290
That's bullying.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:33.123
I go, it's gotta be volunteers.

00:00:33.123 --> 00:00:36.210
And that way I don't get
slapped at the Oscars.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.793
(all laughing)

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:40.644
You know.

00:00:40.644 --> 00:00:42.480
Which, by the way, is
considered an honor now.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.530
Yeah. Can you believe
we lost Gilbert, Saget,

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.296
and Will Smith in the same year?

00:00:48.296 --> 00:00:51.030
(all laughing)

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.550
I mean, gone but not forgotten.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.850
Okay. So here's, I wanna
get your take on this

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.850
because I was in New York the
week Mulaney was hosting SNL

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.270
and he asked me to come
by the Comedy Cellar

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:09.780
and check out his, what he was
gonna do for his monologue.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.570
So I went by and Chris Rock was there.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:13.470
I talked to him for a bit.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:15.660
And then Chris went up,
this might have been

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.560
a week and a half before the famous slap.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:20.393
Right.

00:01:20.393 --> 00:01:23.790
But I saw him deal with
a heckler in the audience

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:25.080
and I thought, wow, that was interesting.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.030
I was there, you know,

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:29.040
I think it was kind of a
very PC person in the crowd

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:29.940
who went, oh, I don't know about that.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.680
And Chris was like, excuse me,

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.900
you don't get to weigh
in on what I'm doing.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.370
And it was really fun to see,
but then to see a week later

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.170
you'd think, well, that might happen at

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.410
a comedy club in Manhattan,
but now he's doing the Oscars.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.380
He's safe. And the idea that a week later

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:47.850
he was on stage getting slapped

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.760
by one of the biggest A-listers
was absolutely stunning

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.650
and something I still
can't believe happened.

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.000
I was in Atlanta. I had
just come off stage,

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.870
watching it on TV.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:59.820
And I'm gonna be sincere here.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.883
I welled up, my eyes welled up.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.660
It was my hero, one of
my heroes, Chris Rock

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.600
arguably my favorite comedian

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.390
getting slapped over a roast joke.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.157
So I saw my whole world and I

00:02:14.157 --> 00:02:17.830
And a throwaway joke,
nothing that in the --

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:20.070
Well, the joke's on Jada

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:24.529
because I have alopecia and
I'm starring in G.I. Jane 2

00:02:24.529 --> 00:02:27.891
(all laughing)

00:02:27.891 --> 00:02:30.630
But it was something

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:31.463
It was something that I --

00:02:31.463 --> 00:02:33.570
Do I slap you? No, I don't.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:36.900
Well, it really showed me,
and I've always been preaching

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.880
like take a joke, have thick skin.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.470
That's been my mantra for years

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.300
since I've been doing the roasts

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.550
and roasts can be very healing.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.520
And I get a lot of
people with disabilities.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.010
I get a lot of just, it's normali--

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.780
roasting is normalizing things

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.640
that aren't necessarily normal.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.140
Maybe not the celebrity roasts

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.750
but like the roasting I do in nightclubs

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.490
and in theaters and stuff.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:04.490
And I got alopecia like
six or seven years ago

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.930
and I did everything I
could to cover it up.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:10.170
You know, my hair fell out
in a course of a few weeks

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.430
and I thought I was dying.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:14.160
I was seeing specialists,
I didn't know what it was.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.140
Took a long time to figure out.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.013
And you know, my eyebrows
look fucked up sometimes

00:03:19.013 --> 00:03:21.810
and this and that, and I was embarrassed

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:24.990
'cause I had this big Jewfro
and I had jokes about it.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.720
And I said, I'll pretend
it's a summer look

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.790
and I'm going on tour and I'll--

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:33.270
And when I saw that
woman not take that joke

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.090
when she could have easily normalized it,

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:40.680
for hundreds of thousands
of kids all over the world

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:44.850
watching this beautiful movie
star on TV, be a bad sport

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:48.000
about something she could
easily have laughed about.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.953
Even if it hurt inside, she could have.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.070
And to me, it forced me to talk about it.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.300
I talked about it on stage.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.850
I talked about it on the
internet the next day,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.950
because it really,

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:04.860
I knew that it was hurting other
people who might have that.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.353
It's mostly kids like imagine being a --

00:04:08.344 --> 00:04:11.940
My doctor, Brett King at
Yale, he's a research doctor.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.130
And he told me about that
he had read about a girl,

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:17.610
12 years old who had
to wear a wig to school

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:19.830
and the kids bullied her
and they pulled her wig off

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:22.320
and she fricking killed herself.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.270
So, to me, anything you
can do to normalize it

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.330
and own it, I guess, and
learn to live with it

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:34.129
and channel your inner rock
star, which for me is Pitbull

00:04:34.129 --> 00:04:35.580
(all laughing)

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.450
If he got attacked by a pit bull,

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:43.170
is what we Jews call a mitzvah.

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:45.033
And not that I'm trying to be,

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.350
listen, I'm sure no doubt for a woman

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:51.270
it's very traumatizing, but she,

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:55.830
Jada had talked about it
so much publicly that for,

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:57.840
it made me think that
it wasn't the alopecia

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.210
that was bothering her, that
maybe she had some history

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:01.890
with Chris Rock.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:05.190
Yeah. To me, not knowing anything,

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:07.530
it felt like there's
something else happening here.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:10.110
And also it's a weird pool shot

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:12.240
because it's not her storming the stage,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.110
it's her signaling to her
husband who then goes into

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.030
but looked to me like a character.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:21.420
Like he reset and became this person

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:22.770
who I'm now gonna go kick ass.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:23.940
And you think, I don't know,

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.417
I've met Will Smith a bunch of times.

00:05:26.417 --> 00:05:27.480
That's not who he is.

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.520
That he went into character.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:30.570
It all felt very strange.

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:32.610
Somebody told me that is who he is.

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:35.640
And we've been watching
a character for 20 years.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.590
So I don't know, man.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:39.000
I don't know. I hope he's okay.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.910
I love Will Smith. I
always enjoyed his movies.

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:44.370
The one time I met him, he
did scare the shit out of me.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.100
I was writing the MTV Awards
with Chris Rock and we

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.070
were standing at the stage.
This is when he was doing Ali.

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.170
And he goes, you know, "Hey, Jeff Ross!"

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:54.150
He just sort of recognized
me as, you know,

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:55.580
and he starts coming at me like Ali,

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.600
boom, boom, boom, boom,
boom. Throwing punches.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:58.813
And I was like, scared shitless.

00:05:58.813 --> 00:06:02.280
I was like, this guy has like
really got the character down.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:05.970
So, he is an icon and

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:09.840
it's sad to see someone's
career plummet like that.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.720
I mean, his, you know, who am I to talk?

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.720
His worst day is probably
my best day in show business

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.449
but it really hurt me to see,

00:06:18.449 --> 00:06:20.700
I said to my girlfriend that night

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:24.000
and I, like, basically
through my emotions, I said,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.657
if he gets away with this,
I'm done in show business.

00:06:26.657 --> 00:06:27.510
Right, right.

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:32.130
Who's gonna stick up
for me at a comedy club

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:33.240
if this guy's getting away

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.734
with slapping Chris Rock on the Oscars?

00:06:35.734 --> 00:06:39.720
And think about it,
like, he slapped Chris,

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:43.680
he won, he made a speech,
he got a standing ovation.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.990
He went to the after party, went dancing,

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:48.240
and then they kicked
him outta the Academy.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.073
I know.

00:06:49.073 --> 00:06:50.490
That's like going to
your favorite restaurant

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.350
for your birthday, they pick up the check,

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.510
the waitress fucks all
your friends and families.

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:57.300
You have three desserts,
then the maitre d's like,

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.117
sir we're gonna have to ask you to leave.

00:06:59.117 --> 00:07:00.630
(all laughing)

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:03.670
Yeah. It's 5:00 AM. My
Dick is wet. I'm leaving.

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:05.423
(all laughing)

00:07:05.423 --> 00:07:06.683
I did it. I had the best time ever.

00:07:06.683 --> 00:07:10.600
That's the greatest
Denny's I've ever heard of.

